Progress status for this period: stalling. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "You missed half your sessions this week — getting back to full attendance is the most important thing right now.",
  "summary": "This was a mixed week. You showed up for Upper A and Lower A, which is a solid foundation, but missing Upper B and Lower B cut your total volume roughly in half compared to a full week. With only 16 sets logged across all muscle groups, it's no surprise that strength indexes have flatlined — none of your lifts moved in either direction. The good news is there's no regression happening, and your tonnage numbers are respectable given the reduced session count. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, your body is well-positioned to respond quickly once you restore full training frequency. The path to building muscle is clear: get back to four sessions next week and trust the process.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "All six muscle groups are stalling this period. Chest held at 104 (no change), back held at 103 (no change), shoulders held at 102 (no change), biceps held at 101 (no change), and triceps held at 103 (no change). Legs actually edged down one point from 106 to 105, which on its own doesn't meet the threshold for regression (a 3-point drop is required), but it is a mild warning sign. No group rose 3 or more points, so the overall status is stalling. The most likely explanation is the missed sessions — Upper B and Lower B were both skipped, meaning chest, back, shoulders, biceps, and triceps each only received one training stimulus instead of two this week. Muscles need sufficient weekly volume and frequency to drive strength gains, and half the planned sessions simply wasn't enough to push any index forward.",
    "status": "stalling"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders account for only 9.4% of total volume split and just 2 sets this week, which is notably lower than chest (3 sets, 24%) and back (3 sets, 25.1%). For balanced upper-body muscle development, shoulders typically benefit from volume closer to that of chest and back. This gap may widen if Upper B sessions (which likely contain additional shoulder work) continue to be missed."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps received only 2 sets and represent just 4.8% of total volume, the lowest of any muscle group tracked. While biceps are a smaller muscle and require less absolute volume than compound-dominant groups, 2 sets per week is on the low end for a muscle-building goal and leaves room to add one or two more sets when full training resumes."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Prioritise attendance above all else next week — aim to complete all four planned sessions (Upper A, Upper B, Lower A, Lower B) before making any other adjustments to your program.",
    "Keep the weights and rep ranges exactly where they are for now; with consistent attendance restored, the strength indexes should start moving upward again without any need to change loading.",
    "Pay particular attention to shoulder volume when you return to Upper B — given the current mild imbalance, ensure shoulder work is not rushed or skipped within that session.",
    "If life pressures make four sessions difficult again, consider consolidating to three full-body sessions rather than dropping two out of a four-day split, so all muscle groups receive at least some stimulus each week."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "All strength indexes stalled (no group moved 3+ points) due to half the planned sessions being missed; full attendance next period is the single highest priority before any other program changes are considered."
}
